---
import { issuesUrl } from "../data/site";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---
<Layout title="Methodology: How Rates and Fees Are Calculated | MathMyRate" description="How MathMyRate calculates rates and fees: integer-cent arithmetic, rounding, official sources, review dates, inverse checks, and corrections.">
  <main id="main-content" class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-16 md:px-8 md:py-24">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
    <h1 class="display mt-4 text-5xl font-bold leading-none md:text-6xl">How the numbers are made.</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink/70">Every result on this site comes from an exact-integer calculation with named assumptions and a public source. Nothing is inferred from a look-alike pricing page, and unsupported cases are refused rather than estimated.</p>

    <section class="mt-12 space-y-10">
      <div>
        <h2 class="display text-2xl font-bold">Money is integer cents, not floating point</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/70">All monetary inputs and outputs are bigint integer cents. Percentages are integer basis points. Divisions round explicitly to the nearest cent, half up, and each fee component is rounded on its own before components are summed. If a provider's published page does not establish a component-rounding policy, the result carries an estimate warning saying exactly that.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="display text-2xl font-bold">Rates come from named, linked sources</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/70">Each fee rule is tied to a specific provider product, channel, account market, and currency, with a link to the official page it came from and the date it was last reviewed. A rule that cannot be traced to a documented scenario is not shipped. Scenarios the sources do not cover, such as the Gumroad transaction that crosses its monthly volume threshold, or Lemon Squeezy orders with collected tax, are labeled unsupported, never filled in with a guessed blend.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="display text-2xl font-bold">Inverse calculations are proven, not asserted</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/70">The keep-a-target mode finds the least gross amount whose separately rounded fee components still reach your target. Because rounding can make the result non-monotonic by a cent, the search recomputes real rounded fees for every candidate, and automated tests brute-force the answer: the returned amount reaches the target, and one cent less does not.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="display text-2xl font-bold">What the calculators do not do</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/70">They do not calculate taxes. Tax percentages are planning reserves you enter. Fee results are net receipts before expenses and income tax, not profit. Freelance results estimate required revenue and minimum rates; they do not determine deductions, progressive taxes, sales tax, or statutory contributions. Currency formatting never converts money or selects a tax jurisdiction.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="display text-2xl font-bold">Embedding and citing the calculators</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/70">Every calculator can be embedded on another site with an iframe, and each calculator page offers the code with a credit link back to the full page. Embedded copies run the same calculations in the visitor's browser, store nothing, and ask search engines not to index them. If you cite a result, link the calculator page itself: its sources, review date, and assumptions are listed there, and the <a class="underline" href="/changelog/">changelog</a> records every rule change.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="display text-2xl font-bold">Review and corrections</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/70">Every fee page shows the date its source was last reviewed. When a provider changes published pricing, the rule's revision is updated independently of that review date, and the change is visible in the page's assumptions and exclusions. If you find a result that disagrees with its own stated assumptions, that is a bug: the correct behavior is to fix the rule and record the correction, not to explain the number away. Report one by <a class="underline" href={issuesUrl}>opening an issue on GitHub</a>, and see the <a class="underline" href="/changelog/">changelog</a> for past rule changes.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
